:: R6_Mixer [r6 story]
<<scene "Meridian, Floor 40 Event Space" "files/images/locations/r6-mixer-event-space.png">>
<<objective "Work the room.">>

The event space on 40 has been dressed for the occasion. Low lighting, high ceilings, a jazz trio in the corner playing something I should probably know the name of. The bar is stocked with the kind of liquor that says we bill at four hundred an hour. Canapes circulate on silver trays carried by servers who are probably actors. This is the corporate theater I've lived inside for six years, and tonight the set feels more artificial than usual. Maybe because I'm carrying my own secret stagecraft under my clothes.

Lena gets a glass of white wine. I get a red. We split up within four minutes, which is either efficient networking or a quiet confession about the state of our marriage. I tell myself it's the former.

<<portrait "files/images/characters/r6-martin-bar.png" "Martin Crane">>

Martin Crane holds court near the bar. Bourbon, no ice, the glass dwarfed in his hand. He's in a charcoal suit that probably cost as much as my car payment, and his laugh is the kind that fills a room whether the room wants to be filled or not. I drift into his orbit because that's what mid-level account directors do. Gravitational hierarchy.

<<say "Martin Crane">>Ashford.<</say>>

Handshake. His grip is a performance of masculinity, every time, knuckles compressed against mine until I feel the bones adjust.

<<say "Martin Crane">>Meet Tatiana from the Lisbon office. She's taking over the Henderson replacement.<</say>>

I shake Tatiana's hand. Hers is firm and cool. We make small talk about Lisbon, about the Henderson transition, about synergies and pipeline alignment, and I nod and contribute and sound competent while the lace panties press against my hardening cock under the table and the bra straps dig into my shoulders every time I gesture. Martin watches me with his usual expression. The one that says he's evaluating whether I'm worth the oxygen I'm consuming in his proximity.

<<say "Martin Crane">>How are things on your end? Settling into the new vertical?<</say>>

<<sayself>>Smoothly. Victoria runs a tight ship.<</sayself>>

<<say "Martin Crane">>Victoria runs a prison ship.<</say>>

He laughs. It's a joke. It's also not a joke. He studies my face for a reaction and I give him the bland professional mask I've been perfecting since business school.

<<sayself>>She's been very supportive.<</sayself>>

The word "supportive" tastes like communion wine in a brothel. I sip my red and excuse myself.

<<portrait "files/images/characters/r6-harper-party.png" "Harper Quinn">>

Harper Quinn is near the appetizer station. She's out of her usual office uniform of structured blazers and pulled-back hair. Tonight she's in a fitted navy dress, simple, cut well, and her dark hair is down around her shoulders. She looks different. Softer. More present. Like a higher resolution version of the person I see across cubicle walls every weekday.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>You keep touching your shoulder.<</say>>

I drop my hand. I'd been adjusting the bra strap. Unconscious, automatic, the kind of micro-gesture that becomes habit when you're wearing hardware nobody can see. My heart rate spikes.

<<sayself>>Old habit.<</sayself>>

<<say "Harper Quinn">>Weird habit.<</say>>

She's smiling, but her eyes are doing that thing they do. That cataloguing thing. Harper Quinn looks at people the way forensic accountants look at spreadsheets. She's searching for the number that doesn't add up. I am the number.

<<sayself>>You look nice.<</sayself>>

<<say "Harper Quinn">>You look nervous.<</say>>

She takes a shrimp from the tray and eats it, watching me.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>Relax, Daniel. It's a party. Nobody's getting fired tonight.<</say>>

She doesn't know how funny that isn't.

<<npc "harper" "suspicion" 3>>

