:: R6_FortyMinutes [r6 story]
<<moodimage "files/images/mood/r6-adrian-lena-window.png" "">>
<<objective "Watch the room.">>

Or she finds him. Or the collision is mutual, two objects in complementary orbits finally reaching the point where physics insists. I don't see the beginning. One moment I'm discussing the Jakarta expansion with a VP from operations, and the next I glance across the room and Lena is standing by the floor-to-ceiling windows with Adrian, and the city is laid out behind them like a backdrop designed specifically for this scene.

They're talking. Not small talk. Not the quick, transactional exchanges that parties run on. They're talking the way people talk when they've forgotten there's anyone else in the room, bodies oriented toward each other like satellite dishes, the rest of the event fading into background noise.

I watch from across the room. Wine warming in my hand. Lace pressing against my cock. Bra straps shifting under my jacket.

Adrian says something and Lena laughs. Not the polite little exhale she gives at dinner when I attempt a joke. A real laugh. Her head goes back, throat exposed, her whole body opening like a door that's been locked. The sound carries across thirty feet of corporate event space and hits me in the chest.

I haven't made her laugh like that in over a year. Maybe longer. Maybe I never did, and I've been misremembering our courtship through the same lens I use to remember my high school body as athletic when it was, at best, adequate.

I sip my wine. I watch.

He remembers the title of a book she mentioned to someone else earlier in the evening. I catch this because I'm standing close enough to hear fragments, drifting through the crowd like a ghost at my own haunting.

<<say "Adrian Wolfe">>Didn't you mention that to Tatiana earlier? The one about the journalist in Beirut?<</say>>

Lena's face does something I can feel from ten feet away. Surprise. Delight. The specific pleasure of being listened to by someone who was listening before you even knew they were there.

He's been paying attention to her before they were introduced. The precision of that is devastating.

They talk about her background. French-Canadian, grew up in Montreal, moved to the States for grad school. He asks questions with genuine curiosity. Not the performative multiculturalism I've seen at a hundred mixers where white partners ask about someone's heritage with the tone of a man ordering from an ethnic restaurant. Adrian asks because he wants to know. Because her answers interest him. Because she interests him.

She touches his arm. Mid-point, illustrating something, her fingers landing on his forearm and staying for two beats longer than necessary. I know that gesture. She used to do it to me. In restaurants, at parties, in the early years when my presence was enough to make her animated. Her fingers on my arm, light and possessive, claiming me in public.

Now she's claiming him. Or he's letting her. Or they're meeting somewhere in the middle where ownership becomes mutual and neither person minds.

Forty minutes. I count. Not consciously at first, but after twenty I start checking my watch the way you check a wound. Twenty-two. Twenty-seven. Thirty-one. They don't move. They don't look at their phones. They don't scan the room for better options. They exist in a private gravitational field and the rest of Meridian's quarterly mixer rotates around them like debris.

<<portrait "files/images/characters/r6-victoria-observing.png" "Victoria Cross">>

Victoria appears beside me. I don't notice her until she speaks.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>Adrian has that effect on people.<</say>>

She's sipping her wine. Following my gaze. Her expression is perfectly composed, but there's something underneath it. Satisfaction. The quiet pleasure of a watchmaker who just heard the second hand click into place.

She doesn't elaborate. She doesn't need to. The tone carries everything. This isn't a surprise to her. This is Tuesday.

I stand there. Wine going warm. Wife illuminated by another man's attention like a painting in a gallery I can no longer afford admission to. Lace panties damp against my cock. Bra straps hidden under my jacket like wires in a wall nobody suspects is wired to explode.

The juxtaposition is so complete it feels architectural. Designed. Like someone drew a blueprint of the most violent contrast a single human body could contain and then built it with me as the material.

My wife is falling into another man's light. I am standing in the dark wearing women's underwear for my female boss. Both things are happening simultaneously. Both things are happening to me. And the feeling underneath it all, the one I can't name because it doesn't have a single name, is not pure jealousy. Not pure shame. Not pure arousal.

It's all of them. At once. Screaming in a chord I don't have the instrument to play.

Victoria sips her wine.

<<sayself>>He's charming.<</sayself>>

My voice sounds like someone else's.

<<say "Victoria Cross">>He is. And your wife looks lovely tonight. That green.<</say>>

She says it the way you'd compliment a painting's frame. Appreciative. Contextual. Like Lena is an element of a composition Victoria is evaluating for balance.

Thirty-eight minutes. Lena throws her head back laughing again. Adrian catches her wine glass before she spills it, and their hands touch around the stem, and neither of them lets go.

Forty minutes. I finish my wine. It tastes like copper and defeat.

